    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1091">

                <pb facs="1452" n="1442" xml:id="pg1442"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/12–545: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-12-05T18:00:00-05:00">December 5, 1945—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5681. For Angell No. 68. Dept believes you should strongly oppose Belgium and
                    Netherlands proposals (reurtel Angell 102<anchor n="9" xml:id="d1091-f1"/>).</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Selection of actual plants must be left to ACC on quadripartite basis
                        since they have responsibility for shaping future German economy. Severe
                        restrictions such as outlined in proposals would retard whole reparation
                        program.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Proposals put premium on pre-war property claims against Germany as
                        opposed to all others. While quantitatively most important claims such as
                        budgetary costs, destruction, deaths, etc. will be settled by a small
                        percentage, under subject proposal pre-war claims to actual German property
                        would be settled nearly 100 percent. Moral aspect also poor as proposal
                        gives undue weight to pre-war trading and investments with Germany over
                        destitution caused by war.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
